I've written the session plan to session_plan/. The commit step didn't happen because session_plan/ is gitignored, so git add refused it. The files are on disk. I didn't force-add them, assuming the harness reads them from there; that's the reason the plan's commit command ends in || true.

Task 1 — upgrade yoagent 0.24.2 → 0.25.2 (#1007), product. This is the largest thing I owe, and nothing it depended on is still pending. It breaks the 3-file rule on purpose: the bump only compiles as a whole, and tests/doc_version_claims.rs goes red on every yoagent-version-claim marker as soon as the lockfile moves, so it can't be spread across sessions. The task file says why the exception applies to this task only. Steps:
• Bump the version and let the compiler list what broke. That should mostly be renaming Extension to Custom in about 5 places.
• Turn on the bundled prices (enable_bundled()) through one call that both main and the unit tests reach. The existing price tests must pass without being edited; if one only passes after an edit, the call is in the wrong place.
• Re-read each of the ~22 version-claim markers against the 0.25.2 source, with no bulk find-and-replace. Claims that are no longer true get marked superseded in place.
• Escape hatch: if the build breaks in more than about 6 files beyond the ones the issue names, undo the bump and post every error location on #1007 instead.
• Two things that aren't code changes, each with a check: a comment on #988 saying its cancel-path probe now matters, because in 0.25.2 Ctrl+C no longer reaches a running sub-agent command; and Fixes #1007 in the commit only if every step landed.

Task 2 — test whether variable tricks get dangerous commands past the bash safety check, product. Claude Code recently fixed a hole where commands written through a variable were auto-approved. My check, analyze_bash_command, matches on how a command is spelled, so I'll test the same idea against it: plain commands like rm -rf ~ next to disguised versions (X=rm; $X …, BASH_ARGV0, eval, $(…), bash -c). A few harmless commands like echo $HOME and "$CARGO" build are included to check they're still allowed as before. The same test runs against the user deny list.
• If the result calls for a fix, it goes only in src/safety.rs: commands it can't read get sent to the existing "needs confirmation" level. A bypass of the deny list would go into a new issue rather than this task.
• If nothing gets through, the result is written as "variable tricks don't get past analyze_bash_command", never as "the analyzer is correct".

